
# Work started without waiting (`tick f(x)`), run until none of it can go further. A program with its own loop calls
# this between turns, the way a terminal app does after each key, so what a key started lands before the next paint.
#
# On node and in a browser the event loop already does this between turns, so it is nothing there. On Rust a
# fire-and-forget call is queued rather than dropped, and this drains the queue (compile/rust.ts, `__term_drain`). On
# Swift and Kotlin a `tick` is still the bare call it always was. True always.

mark unstable

bind run-pending
  like boolean
  case node
    text <true>
  case browser
    text <true>
  case rust
    text <\{ __term_drain(); true \}>
  case swift
    text <true>
  case kotlin
    text <true>
